@* HOW TO READ THIS: the decoder *@
<dialog class="story-doc" id="storyRead" aria-labelledby="storyReadTitle">
    <header class="doc-head">
        <div>
            <p class="doc-kicker">The decoder</p>
            <h2 id="storyReadTitle">How to read this</h2>
        </div>
        <button type="button" class="doc-close" aria-label="Close">×</button>
    </header>
    <div class="doc-body">
        <h3>It runs in order</h3>
        <p>This page is one story, told top to bottom. <b>01 Dallas</b> is growing up, <b>02 Leaving home</b> is the move to McCombs, <b>03 Austin</b> is the work, and <b>04 Home</b> is everything a résumé can’t hold. You can still skip ahead: the menu, the rail and <kbd class="key">/</kbd> all go anywhere.</p>

        <h3>What the marks mean</h3>
        <dl class="legend">
            <dt><span class="legend-band">Dallas</span></dt>
            <dd><b>A dark band</b> opens each chapter. The bands are narrated (“she”). Everything between them is me talking (“I”).</dd>
            <dt><span class="legend-num">240+</span></dt>
            <dd><b>A big number</b> is always a real one, never an adjective. Hover it to see where it comes from, and click it to fly to the proof. It counts up from zero the first time you see it.</dd>
            <dt><span class="legend-dots"><i style="background: var(--thread-people)"></i><i style="background: var(--thread-make)"></i><i style="background: var(--thread-start)"></i></span></dt>
            <dd><b>A colored dot</b> marks a thread, one of three things that show up in every chapter. Hover a number and everything else on its thread lights up, in every chapter.</dd>
            <dt><span class="legend-rail"><i class="on"></i><i class="on"></i><i></i><i></i></span></dt>
            <dd><b>The rail</b> on the left edge of wide screens shows which chapter you’re in. Hover a thread at its bottom to see which chapters it runs through. Click it to keep it lit while you scroll.</dd>
        </dl>

        <h3>The three threads</h3>
        <ul class="thread-list">
            <li data-thread-key="people"><span><b>Understanding people.</b> Why people choose what they choose: psychology, marketing, teaching, the clients I’ve worked for.</span></li>
            <li data-thread-key="make"><span><b>Making things.</b> A children’s book, a rideshare system, a data warehouse, this site.</span></li>
            <li data-thread-key="start"><span><b>Starting things.</b> Two Girls Who Code chapters, and anything else that needed someone to go first.</span></li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Where the numbers come from</h3>
        <p>Career numbers come from the same records as my résumé and the sections they point to. Music numbers come from <a href="@heavyRotation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Heavy Rotation ↗</a>, my Spotify data warehouse: 182,293 raw records cleaned into a PostgreSQL star schema, and every number computed by its export, not typed by hand.</p>

        <h3>Keys</h3>
        <p><kbd class="key">/</kbd> or <kbd class="key">⌘K</kbd> to search, <kbd class="key">?</kbd> for this guide, <kbd class="key">esc</kbd> to close anything. Cards flip when you tap them.</p>
    </div>
</dialog>

@* HOW I BUILT THIS: the case study *@
<dialog class="story-doc" id="storyBuild" aria-labelledby="storyBuildTitle">
    <header class="doc-head">
        <div>
            <p class="doc-kicker">Case study</p>
            <h2 id="storyBuildTitle">How I built this</h2>
        </div>
        <button type="button" class="doc-close" aria-label="Close">×</button>
    </header>
    <div class="doc-body">
        <h3>The problem</h3>
        <p>The old home page was organized like a résumé: work, then school, then awards, then me. Accurate, and easy to forget. A story has a beginning, a middle and an end, so I rebuilt the page around one: where I started, the move, the work, and who I am now. The facts didn’t change. The order did.</p>

        <h3>By the numbers</h3>
        <div class="build-stats">
            <div><b>@chapters.Length</b><span>chapters, in the order they happened</span></div>
            <div><b>@chapters.Sum(c => c.Stats.Length)</b><span>numbers, each linked to its source</span></div>
            <div><b>0</b><span>front-end frameworks or build steps</span></div>
            <div><b>12 h</b><span>how long live Spotify results stay cached</span></div>
            <div><b>60</b><span>music requests per visitor per minute, at most</span></div>
        </div>

        <h3>How a number gets on the page</h3>
        <ol class="build-flow">
            <li><b>A record in C#</b><span>Each chapter is a <code>StoryChapter</code> record at the top of <code>Index.cshtml</code>, and each number is a <code>StoryStat</code> carrying its source, a link to the proof and its thread.</span></li>
            <li><b>Rendered on the server</b><span>ASP.NET Core MVC turns the records into HTML with Razor, so every number is real text on first paint: readable without JavaScript, by screen readers and by search engines.</span></li>
            <li><b>Brought to life in the browser</b><span><code>story.js</code> counts the numbers up, tilts them toward your cursor, draws the thread connections and runs the rail, in plain JavaScript.</span></li>
            <li><b>Music numbers from a warehouse</b><span>182,293 raw Spotify records go through a Python ETL into a PostgreSQL star schema, and a 680 KB export feeds Heavy Rotation and the numbers here.</span></li>
        </ol>

        <h3>Decisions worth explaining</h3>
        <p><b>Search builds itself from the page.</b> Press <kbd class="key">/</kbd> and the index is assembled from the chapters, numbers, roles, projects, classes and headings actually on the page, so nothing has to be registered by hand. Results score best at the start of a label, then the start of a word, then letters in order, so “gwc” finds Girls Who Code. If a result sits inside a closed tab or the tucked-away “a little more about me” section, search opens it first, then flies there.</p>
        <p><b>The opening never gets in the way.</b> It plays once per visit. Any key, click or scroll skips it, and it never plays for a link to a section, a background tab, or anyone who has asked their device for less motion. The cover’s own entrance waits until the curtain lifts.</p>
        <p><b>Live data, politely.</b> This server is also the API for my live Spotify data. Access tokens are cached and renewed a minute before they expire, behind a lock so only one request refreshes at a time. Results are cached for 12 hours, MusicBrainz genre lookups stay at one a second, and each visitor gets 60 music requests a minute.</p>
        <p><b>Shipped like a product.</b> .NET 10 and C#, a two-stage Docker build, deployed to Render on every push to <code>main</code>.</p>
        <p><a href="https://github.com/suhxnitiwari" target="_blank" rel="noopener">See the code on GitHub ↗</a></p>
    </div>
</dialog>

<script src="~/js/story.js" asp-append-version="true"></script>



